# the llama who chases the dogs

**Attention from scratch, in Elixir.**

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:kino_livereload, "~> 0.1"},
    {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"},
    {:tiny_llm, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")}
  ],
  # The path dependency is compiled once per runtime and cached, so without
  # this a change under lib/ is invisible until the runtime is restarted.
  force: true
)
```

## What this is

A complete decoder-only transformer language model in pure Elixir standard
library. No Nx, no Axon, no hex packages in the model code. Hand-derived
backpropagation. About thirteen thousand parameters, trained on a laptop CPU
in under a minute.

This notebook is the talk, in talk order. Kino and VegaLite appear here for
*presentation only*; they never touch the math.

**Built: stages 1 through 7.** The vocabulary, the grammar that writes our
training data, the math library, and then four models in a row: a bigram that
counts, a bigram that learns, a single attention head that can finally look
back further than one word, and the full block that wraps it. The bigram is the baseline the transformer has to beat, and by the
end of this notebook you will see exactly where it fails, why, and what
replaces it.

```elixir
alias TinyLlm.Attention
alias TinyLlm.Bigram
alias TinyLlm.Embedder
alias TinyLlm.Eval
alias TinyLlm.Grammar
alias TinyLlm.Transformer
alias TinyLlm.PCA
alias TinyLlm.Sampler
alias TinyLlm.Tensor
alias TinyLlm.Train
alias TinyLlm.Vocab
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

# Two horizontal lines every loss chart in this notebook carries.
#
# ln(32) is what a uniform guess over 32 words costs, so it is where a model
# that has learned nothing sits. 1.9021 is H(next | previous) for this grammar,
# computed from the grammar rather than measured: the best score attainable
# seeing only the previous word, whatever the model.
reference_lines =
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.data_from_values([
    %{loss: :math.log(32), bound: "knowing nothing: ln(32)"},
    %{loss: 1.9021, bound: "best possible seeing one word"}
  ])
  |> Vl.mark(:rule, stroke_dash: [6, 4], size: 2)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "loss", type: :quantitative)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:color, "bound",
    type: :nominal,
    scale: [range: ["#9e9e9e", "#d95f02"]],
    legend: [title: nil, orient: :bottom]
  )

:ok
```

## The entire vocabulary

Thirty-two words. One word is one token is one integer. There is no
tokenizer anywhere in this project, which means there is nothing hidden
between the text you read and the numbers the model sees.

The ordering is deliberate: every word class occupies a contiguous range of
ids. That choice pays off the moment we draw a picture.

```elixir
# Nouns are split by number so no row is long enough for the table to
# truncate it. The combined row ran to ten words and lost the last three
# behind the column width.
[
  {"determiners", Vocab.determiners()},
  {"nouns, singular", Vocab.nouns(:singular)},
  {"nouns, plural", Vocab.nouns(:plural)},
  {"transitive verbs", Vocab.transitive_verbs()},
  {"intransitive verbs", Vocab.intransitive_verbs()},
  {"copula", Vocab.copula()},
  {"adjectives", Vocab.adjectives()},
  {"connectives", Vocab.connectives()},
  {"boundaries", [Vocab.start_token(), Vocab.end_token()]}
]
|> Enum.map(fn {class, words} ->
  ids = Enum.map(words, &Vocab.word_to_id/1)

  %{
    "class" => class,
    "ids" => Enum.join(ids, " "),
    "words" => Enum.join(words, " ")
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "32 words, grouped by class")
```

A word is only ever its id, and back again:

```elixir
sentence = ~w(the big llama sees a mouse .)

[
  words: sentence,
  ids: Vocab.encode(sentence),
  back_again: sentence |> Vocab.encode() |> Vocab.decode()
]
```

## We own the training data

Most of what makes language models hard to study is that nobody knows what is
in the training set. So we write ours with a probabilistic grammar, which makes
the ground truth knowable: we can ask whether the model learned subject-verb
agreement, rather than guessing.

```
Sentence        -> NounPhrase VerbPhrase "."

NounPhrase      -> Determiner AdjectivePhrase Noun (70%)
                 | NounPhrase "and" NounPhrase (15%)
                 | Determiner AdjectivePhrase Noun "who" VerbPhrase (15%)

AdjectivePhrase -> nothing (55%)
                 | Adjective (30%)
                 | Adjective Adjective (15%)

VerbPhrase      -> IntransitiveVerb (35%)
                 | TransitiveVerb NounPhrase (35%)
                 | Copula Adjective (30%)
```

Agreement is enforced while sampling, so every sentence is grammatical by
construction. A singular-or-plural flag is decided once, when a noun phrase
picks its noun, then travels up to the caller and back down into the verb.

```elixir
Grammar.seed(42)

Grammar.corpus(12)
|> Enum.map_join("\n", &Enum.join(&1, " "))
|> Kino.Text.new()
```

### The sentence this whole talk is about

```
the llama who chases the dogs flees
    ^^^^^                     ^^^^^   subject and verb, both singular
                         ^^^^         distractor: plural, and nearest the verb
```

Fifteen percent of subjects take a relative clause, which drops a **distractor
noun** between the subject and its verb. The verb still has to agree with the
head noun, `llama`, and not with `dogs` sitting right beside it.

Anything predicting agreement from the nearest noun answers `flee` and is
wrong. Getting this right means knowing that `llama` is the subject and `dogs`
is merely the object of a subordinate clause. That is the entire task.

Linguists call this **agreement attraction**, and it is a standard probe for
whether a model has learned syntax or surface statistics. Humans fail it too:
"the key to the cabinets *were* rusty" is an error fluent speakers produce.

The grammar generates this shape constantly, and it is easy to find because a
plural noun immediately followed by a singular verb can only happen when that
verb agrees with something further back:

```elixir
singular_verbs =
  Vocab.transitive_verbs(:singular) ++
    Vocab.intransitive_verbs(:singular) ++ Vocab.copula(:singular)

distractor_case? = fn words ->
  words
  |> Enum.chunk_every(2, 1, :discard)
  |> Enum.any?(fn [first, second] ->
    first in Vocab.nouns(:plural) and second in singular_verbs
  end)
end

Grammar.seed(1234)

Grammar.corpus(20_000)
|> Enum.filter(distractor_case?)
|> Enum.take(8)
|> Enum.map_join("\n", &Enum.join(&1, " "))
|> Kino.Text.new()
```

Every one of those is a sentence where the last noun before the verb disagrees
with it. They are rare, about 1.3% of the corpus, which matters later: the
stage 6 evaluation has to be scored on these specifically. Score it on all
relative clauses instead and a model guessing from the nearest noun collects
roughly half the points by accident.

## A bigram: the dumbest model that works

Count which word follows which, normalize each row, sample. No parameters
learned by gradient descent, no notion of a subject, and no memory beyond one
word back.

```elixir
Grammar.seed(20_250_807)

corpus = Grammar.corpus(2_000)
counts = Bigram.counts(corpus)
matrix = Bigram.matrix(counts)

[
  sentences: length(corpus),
  distinct_pairs_observed: map_size(counts),
  possible_pairs: Vocab.size() * Vocab.size(),
  matrix_shape: "#{length(matrix)} x #{length(hd(matrix))}"
]
```

### The heatmap

Row = the word you just saw. Column = the word that comes next. Each row is a
probability distribution, so each row sums to 1.

Both axes are the full vocabulary in id order, which is why the grammar's rules
show up as **blocks** rather than as noise.

```elixir
words = Vocab.words()

cells =
  for {row, previous_id} <- Enum.with_index(matrix),
      {probability, next_id} <- Enum.with_index(row) do
    %{
      "previous" => Vocab.id_to_word(previous_id),
      "next" => Vocab.id_to_word(next_id),
      "probability" => probability
    }
  end

Vl.new(width: 620, height: 620, title: "P(next word | previous word)", background: "white")
|> Vl.data_from_values(cells)
|> Vl.mark(:rect)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "next",
  type: :nominal,
  sort: words,
  axis: [title: "next word", label_angle: -45]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "previous",
  type: :nominal,
  sort: words,
  axis: [title: "previous word"]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:color, "probability",
  type: :quantitative,
  # White at zero rather than the pale tint `scheme: "blues"` starts from, so
  # the 900-odd empty cells drop out instead of painting the whole square.
  # Square root rather than linear because one cell is 0.9 and most are under
  # 0.05, and a linear ramp spends its entire range on the outlier.
  scale: [type: :sqrt, range: ["#ffffff", "#08306b"]],
  legend: [title: "P"]
)
```

### Reading it

Every bright block is a grammar rule you can point at. These are checks, not
claims:

```elixir
successors = fn word ->
  matrix
  |> Enum.at(Vocab.word_to_id(word))
  |> Enum.with_index()
  |> Enum.reject(fn {probability, _id} -> probability == 0.0 end)
  |> Enum.map(fn {_probability, id} -> Vocab.id_to_word(id) end)
  |> Enum.sort()
end

[Vocab.start_token(), "is", "are", "sees", "chases"]
|> Enum.map(fn word ->
  %{"after" => word, "can only be followed by" => Enum.join(successors.(word), " ")}
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "the grammar, visible in the counts")
```

`<start>` is followed only by determiners, because every sentence begins with
one. The copula is followed only by adjectives, which is `Copula Adjective`
with nothing else possible. Transitive verbs are followed only by determiners,
because they always take an object noun phrase.

And two white stripes mark the sequence boundaries: nothing precedes `<start>`,
so its column is empty, and nothing follows `.`, so its row is empty.

```elixir
row_sums = Enum.map(matrix, &Enum.sum/1)
distributions = Enum.reject(row_sums, &(&1 == 0.0))

[
  {"start token column, nothing precedes a start",
   matrix |> Enum.map(&Enum.at(&1, Vocab.word_to_id(Vocab.start_token()))) |> Enum.sum()},
  {"empty rows, nothing follows an end", Enum.count(row_sums, &(&1 == 0.0))},
  {"rows that are probability distributions", length(distributions)},
  {"of those, summing to exactly 1.0", Enum.count(distributions, &(&1 == 1.0))},
  {"largest deviation from 1.0", distributions |> Enum.map(&abs(&1 - 1.0)) |> Enum.max()}
]
|> Enum.map(fn {measure, value} -> %{"measure" => measure, "value" => value} end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "every row is a distribution, except one")
```

Note that not every row sums to *exactly* 1.0. Nine of them are one unit in
the last place away, about `2.2e-16`, because adding thirty-two floats in a
different order gives a slightly different answer.

That is worth seeing rather than rounding away. It is why the tests compare
row sums with a tolerance instead of `==`, and it is the same reason the
gradient checks in stage 3 onward will use relative error rather than exact
equality. Numerics written directly against floats live with this
everywhere.

### Where the bigram wins

Here is the surprise. The bigram gets adjacent agreement **mostly right, for
free**, with no notion of number anywhere in it. After a singular noun it
expects a singular verb; after a plural noun, a plural one. It learned that
purely from counting.

```elixir
singular_verbs = ~w(sees chases ignores flees is)
plural_verbs = ~w(see chase ignore flee are)

verb_mass = fn word ->
  entries =
    matrix
    |> Enum.at(Vocab.word_to_id(word))
    |> Enum.with_index()
    |> Enum.map(fn {probability, id} -> {probability, Vocab.id_to_word(id)} end)

  mass = fn forms ->
    entries |> Enum.filter(fn {_p, w} -> w in forms end) |> Enum.map(&elem(&1, 0)) |> Enum.sum()
  end

  {mass.(singular_verbs), mass.(plural_verbs)}
end

~w(llama llamas dog dogs mouse mice)
|> Enum.map(fn word ->
  {singular, plural} = verb_mass.(word)
  agreeing = if word in Vocab.nouns(:plural), do: plural, else: singular

  %{
    "after" => word,
    "singular verb mass" => Float.round(singular, 3),
    "plural verb mass" => Float.round(plural, 3),
    "share that agrees" => "#{Float.round(agreeing / (singular + plural) * 100, 1)}%"
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "verb mass following each noun")
```

### Where it cannot

Look at the share that *disagrees*. It is not noise, and it is not evenly
spread. It is exactly the two constructions in our grammar that break
adjacency:

* A plural verb after a **singular** noun comes from a compound subject:
  `the goose and the llama flee`. The pair `llama flee` is real.
* A singular verb after a **plural** noun comes from a relative clause:
  `the llama who chases the dogs flees`. The pair `dogs flees` is real, and
  that sentence is the distractor case itself.

So the disagreeing mass in each row is a fingerprint of the very sentences the
bigram cannot handle. Restricted to the two candidate forms, here is what it
answers after `dogs`:

```elixir
{singular, plural} = verb_mass.("dogs")

[
  {"context", "the llama who chases the dogs ___"},
  {"correct answer", "singular, agreeing with llama"},
  {"bigram answers plural with confidence",
   "#{Float.round(plural / (singular + plural) * 100, 1)}%"},
  {"single most likely next token overall",
   matrix |> Enum.at(Vocab.word_to_id("dogs")) |> Tensor.argmax() |> Vocab.id_to_word()}
]
|> Enum.map(fn {measure, value} -> %{"measure" => measure, "value" => value} end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "what the bigram answers after 'dogs'")
```

The bigram conditions on one word, and that word is `dogs`. It will answer
plural essentially every time, and it will be wrong essentially every time.

```
the llama who chases the dogs ___
                         ^^^^         the only thing a bigram can see
    ^^^^^                             the only thing that matters
```

It does not fail at agreement in general. It fails **precisely and only** when
a distractor sits between a subject and its verb, because there is no row in
this matrix for "the subject, four words ago."

That missing row is what attention is for.

### What it generates

Sample from the start row, follow the chain, stop at a period.

Every adjacent pair below is locally plausible, because every adjacent pair was
observed. The sentences are still nonsense, because nothing tracks a subject
for longer than one word.

```elixir
Bigram.seed(7)

1..12
|> Enum.map_join("\n", fn _draw -> matrix |> Bigram.sentence() |> Enum.join(" ") end)
|> Kino.Text.new()
```

## A neural bigram: the same context, learned

The counting bigram is a lookup table. This is the same model with the counts
replaced by parameters, and it is where gradients enter the project.

Two matrices. Look up the previous word's row in a 32 by 32 embedding table,
multiply by a 32 by 32 projection, softmax the result. Cross-entropy loss, and
a gradient that collapses to one line: `dlogits = p - one_hot(target)`.

```elixir
embedder =
  Train.run(%Train.Config{model: Embedder, learning_rate: 1.0, steps: 800, log_every: 100})

points = Enum.map(embedder.losses, fn {step, loss} -> %{step: step, loss: loss} end)

Vl.new(width: 640, height: 280, title: "neural bigram, held-out loss", background: "white")
|> Vl.layers([
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.data_from_values(points)
  |> Vl.mark(:line, point: true)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "step", type: :quantitative)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "loss", type: :quantitative, scale: [zero: false]),
  reference_lines
])
```

It starts on the grey line, `ln(32)`, which is what guessing uniformly over 32
words costs and therefore where a model that has learned nothing sits. It ends
just above the orange one, around 1.944.

**And that is the whole point of this section.** 1.9021 is the conditional
entropy of this grammar given one word of context: the best score any model can
get seeing only the previous word, however many parameters it has. The counting
bigram is already at that ceiling, because counting is the optimal way to use
one word. Learning the same thing with gradients buys essentially nothing.

So the gap between 1.944 and where we want to be is not a training problem, a
size problem, or an optimizer problem. It is an **information** problem. The
model cannot see `llama`, so no amount of learning will make it agree with it.

What this file does have is the two ends of a transformer. An embedding lookup
on the front and a projection to vocabulary logits on the back is exactly what
the full model does at each end. Everything in the next section goes *between*
them.

## One attention head, and nothing else

The neural bigram could not see `llama`. This one can.

`TinyLlm.Attention` is a single causal self-attention head with a projection on
the end and nothing else: no residual, no normalization, no MLP. Every position
emits a query saying what it is looking for and a key saying what it offers, and
the softmax over their dot products decides how much each position draws on each
earlier one.

```elixir
head =
  Train.run(%Train.Config{
    model: Attention,
    batch_size: 8,
    steps: 500,
    learning_rate: 0.5,
    learning_rate_schedule: :cosine,
    log_every: 100
  })

points = Enum.map(head.losses, fn {step, loss} -> %{step: step, loss: loss} end)

Vl.new(width: 640, height: 320, title: "one attention head, held-out loss", background: "white")
|> Vl.layers([
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.data_from_values(points)
  |> Vl.mark(:line, point: true)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "step", type: :quantitative)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "loss", type: :quantitative, scale: [zero: false]),
  reference_lines
])
```

Between step 200 and step 300 the line crosses the orange one, and that orange
line is the whole reason this section exists. It is not a benchmark anyone chose. It is the
conditional entropy of this grammar given one word of context, computed from the
grammar itself, and it is the floor for every model in the two sections above no
matter how large.

Going below it is proof, not evidence. The only way to score there is to use
information that is not in the previous word.

One caution worth carrying into the next section, because it cost a day to
learn. **Crossing that floor and handling a distractor are not the same
milestone, and they are hundreds of steps apart.** Measured on this exact
configuration:

| steps | held-out loss | agreement, distractor probes |
| ----- | ------------- | ---------------------------- |
| 100   | 2.5909        | 52.8%                        |
| 200   | 1.9627        | 57.9%                        |
| 300   | 1.7883        | 67.3%                        |
| 400   | 1.7116        | 70.4%                        |
| 500   | 1.6714        | 71.7%                        |

At step 200 the loss is all but at the floor and the model is still at 57.9%,
barely above the coin flip a bigram manages. Agreement keeps climbing for
another three hundred steps after the loss curve has visibly flattened. It buys
those first nats from adjectives and clause structure, and learns agreement
last.

So a loss number is never evidence about agreement. That is what the eval
section is for.

## Adding the rest of the block

The head above is one linear map dressed up. What turns it into a layer you
could stack twelve of is the three things around it: a residual, RMSNorm, and a
32 to 128 to 32 MLP.
Fifteen thousand parameters. Every gradient in it is written out
explicitly, derived analytically in `docs/backprop.md` rather than produced
by autodiff, and checked against finite differences by the test suite.

Batch size 8 with the learning rate decayed on a cosine, both chosen by
measurement rather than taste: see the stage 5 section of `docs/build-brief.md`
for the sweep, and for the finding that one run of this model is worth about
±0.05 nats, so no two configurations may be compared on a single seed.

The loop below is `Train.run/1` unrolled, so the chart can be fed as it goes.
Nothing else about it differs. It renders the chart first and then fills it in,
so expect an empty pair of axes for a few seconds. Roughly a minute in total.

```elixir
config = %Train.Config{
  model: Transformer,
  batch_size: 8,
  steps: 500,
  learning_rate: 0.5,
  learning_rate_schedule: :cosine,
  seed: 1234
}

# Rendered before the loop rather than returned after it, so the points
# arrive while you watch instead of all at once at the end.
chart =
  Vl.new(width: 640, height: 320, title: "the whole model, held-out loss", background: "white")
  |> Vl.layers([
    Vl.new()
    # Named, because a layered chart has more than one dataset and push/3 has
    # to be told which of them a point belongs to.
    |> Vl.data_from_values([], name: "losses")
    |> Vl.mark(:line, point: true)
    |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "step", type: :quantitative)
    |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "loss", type: :quantitative, scale: [zero: false]),
    reference_lines
  ])
  |> Kino.VegaLite.new()
  |> Kino.render()

Train.seed(config.seed)
training = config.training_corpus_size |> Grammar.corpus() |> Transformer.examples()
evaluation = config.evaluation_corpus_size |> Grammar.corpus() |> Transformer.examples()

trained =
  Enum.reduce(0..config.steps, Transformer.init(config), fn step, params ->
    if rem(step, 50) == 0 do
      Kino.VegaLite.push(chart, %{step: step, loss: Transformer.loss(params, evaluation)},
        dataset: "losses"
      )
    end

    Train.step(
      Transformer,
      params,
      Train.batch(training, config.batch_size),
      Train.learning_rate(config, step)
    )
  end)

Kino.VegaLite.push(chart, %{step: config.steps, loss: Transformer.loss(trained, evaluation)},
  dataset: "losses"
)
Transformer.loss(trained, evaluation)
```

This time the line goes well under the orange one and stays there. Both bigrams
in the sections above are stuck at it, and this model finishes about 0.3 nats
below, which is only possible using information the previous word does not
carry.

## What the embeddings learned

Thirty-two dimensions cannot be drawn, so we take the two directions the words
vary most along. `TinyLlm.PCA` finds them by power iteration: multiply by the
covariance matrix, normalize, repeat. Twenty lines, no library, and the same
algorithm that ranked the early web.

```elixir
PCA.seed(1)

classify = fn word ->
  cond do
    word in Vocab.nouns(:singular) -> "singular noun"
    word in Vocab.nouns(:plural) -> "plural noun"
    word in Vocab.adjectives() -> "adjective"
    word in Vocab.determiners() -> "determiner"
    word in ~w(sees chases ignores flees is) -> "singular verb"
    word in ~w(see chase ignore flee are) -> "plural verb"
    true -> "other"
  end
end

scatter =
  trained.embeddings
  |> PCA.project()
  |> Enum.zip(Vocab.words())
  |> Enum.map(fn {{x, y}, word} -> %{x: x, y: y, word: word, class: classify.(word)} end)

Vl.new(width: 640, height: 480, title: "the embedding table, projected")
|> Vl.data_from_values(scatter)
|> Vl.layers([
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.mark(:circle, size: 120, opacity: 0.85)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "x", type: :quantitative)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "y", type: :quantitative)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:color, "class", type: :nominal),
  Vl.new()
  |> Vl.mark(:text, dy: -12, fontSize: 11)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "x", type: :quantitative)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "y", type: :quantitative)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:text, "word", type: :nominal)
])
```

Nobody labelled these words. The only thing the model was ever asked to do was
predict the next one, and parts of speech fell out of it: nouns, adjectives and
determiners land in tight, separate groups, and the first component is close to
a noun detector, every noun on one side and everything else on the other.

Verbs do **not** cluster, and that is not a failure of the picture. Measured,
their singular-to-plural offsets in the embedding table point in no shared
direction at all, averaging a pairwise cosine of 0.105 against a random
baseline of 0.146. In the *projection* matrix the same offsets average 0.487.

Verb number lives only in how a verb is written, never in how it is read, and
the grammar is why: nothing that follows a verb depends on its number, so there
is no reason to encode it on the way in. Nouns carry theirs in both tables,
because a noun's number governs the verb that comes next.

That is also the reason the embedding table and the unembedding are separate
parameters here rather than tied. They are not storing the same thing.

## Where a position looks

The attention matrix for the sentence the whole talk is about. Row `t` is a
distribution over the positions `t` is allowed to see; everything above the
diagonal is exactly zero, because `exp(-1.0e9)` underflows to a true zero.

```elixir
probe = ~w(<start> the llama who chases the dogs)

heatmap = fn params, words ->
  cells =
    params
    |> Transformer.weights(Vocab.encode(words))
    |> Enum.with_index()
    |> Enum.flat_map(fn {row, query} ->
      Enum.with_index(row, fn weight, key ->
        %{query: "#{query} #{Enum.at(words, query)}", key: "#{key} #{Enum.at(words, key)}", weight: weight}
      end)
    end)

  Vl.new(width: 480, height: 480, title: Enum.join(words, " "), background: "white")
  |> Vl.data_from_values(cells)
  |> Vl.mark(:rect)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "key", type: :nominal, sort: nil, title: "attends to")
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "query", type: :nominal, sort: nil, title: "position")
  |> Vl.encode_field(:color, "weight",
    type: :quantitative,
    # White at zero, so the masked upper triangle reads as genuinely absent
    # rather than as a very small number. It is exactly 0.0.
    scale: [domain: [0.0, 1.0], range: ["#ffffff", "#08306b"]],
    legend: [title: "weight"]
  )
end

heatmap.(trained, probe)
```

Two things in that picture are worth stopping on, and neither is the thing you
would expect.

**The first verb attends almost entirely to `<start>`.** That position has
nothing useful to look back at, so it dumps its attention on the one token that
carries no information. This is an *attention sink*, a documented behaviour of
production transformers, appearing unprompted in fifteen thousand parameters.

**The `who` row has gathered the subject.** It spends most of its attention on
`llama`, so its output is a summary of the head noun. And the position that
predicts the main verb spends most of *its* attention on `who`, which sounds
like the model solving agreement in two hops.

It is not, and this is the honest part. With one block, both hops happen at the
same time. The value vector at `who` is built from `who`'s own input,
`embedding("who") + P[3]`, which is identical whichever noun heads the sentence.
Over half the verb position's attention goes somewhere that cannot tell singular
from plural.

The model has built half of a route it cannot use. A second block would read the
first block's output at `who` and get the subject for free. **That is what depth
buys**, and the model drew the argument for it unprompted.

## The numbers that settle it

Held-out `who`-clause sentences with the main verb removed. `the llama who
chases the dogs ___` needs a singular verb agreeing with `llama`, four words
back, while the adjacent noun `dogs` is plural.

```elixir
Grammar.seed(1234)
training_sentences = Grammar.corpus(config.training_corpus_size)
seen = MapSet.new(training_sentences)
counted = training_sentences |> Bigram.counts() |> Bigram.matrix()

Grammar.seed(77)
probes = Grammar.corpus(3_000) |> Enum.reject(&MapSet.member?(seen, &1)) |> Eval.probes()

scores = [
  {"bigram, counted", Eval.bigram_predictor(counted)},
  {"embedder, learned", Eval.embedder_predictor(embedder.params)},
  {"model, one block", Eval.model_predictor(trained)}
]

Kino.DataTable.new(
  Enum.map(scores, fn {label, predict} ->
    %{model: label, "agreement accuracy" => "#{Float.round(Eval.agreement(predict, probes) * 100, 1)}%"}
  end)
)
```

Read that table carefully, because the aggregate flatters the model and a
sceptic in the room will find the reason before you do.

A third of those probes put the relative clause's own verb immediately before
the blank, as in `the llama who flees ___`, and that verb already agrees with
the head. One word of context is enough, and all three models score 100% on
that block. It is the whole of the bigram's score.

The row that matters is the one where a distractor noun intervenes. There the
bigram scores 55.3% and the neural bigram scores 55.3%, to the decimal: they see
the same single word and give the same answer. The model reaches 77.4%.

The claim to make is not "only the model can do agreement". It is **"only the
model does better than chance when a distractor gets in the way"**.

## Generating, and the knob

Temperature divides the logits before the softmax. Below 1 the gaps widen and
the model commits; above 1 they narrow and it wanders. At 0 it is `argmax`.

```elixir
temperature = Kino.Input.range("temperature", min: 0.0, max: 3.0, step: 0.1, default: 1.0)
```

```elixir
Kino.listen(temperature, fn %{value: value} ->
  Sampler.seed(1234)

  sentences =
    trained
    |> Sampler.stream(temperature: value)
    |> Enum.take(200)

  sample = sentences |> Enum.take(8) |> Enum.map_join("\n", &Enum.join(&1, " "))

  IO.puts("""
  temperature #{value}
    grammatical  #{Float.round(Eval.grammaticality(sentences) * 100, 1)}%
    distinct     #{Float.round(length(Enum.uniq(sentences)) / length(sentences) * 100, 1)}%
    unseen       #{Float.round(Eval.novelty(sentences, training_sentences) * 100, 1)}%

  #{sample}
  """)
end)
```

Move the slider and watch the two numbers trade against each other. At 0.0 the
model is 100% grammatical and 0.5% distinct: it says one sentence, forever, and
that sentence is not in the training corpus. At 1.0 it is 89.5% grammatical and
90.5% distinct. Past 2.0 it comes apart, and it comes apart in layers, with the
outermost structure going first: `a llama is dog` keeps the syntax and loses the
sense, `sleepy fast goose dogs flee` has lost the determiners.

## One step, in full

Every distribution the model passed through while writing one sentence. This is
the entire mechanism: ask "what comes next", believe the answer, append it, ask
again.

```elixir
Sampler.seed(20)

steps =
  trained
  |> Sampler.trace()
  |> Enum.with_index()
  |> Enum.flat_map(fn {step, index} ->
    step.distribution
    |> Enum.zip(Vocab.words())
    |> Enum.reject(fn {probability, _word} -> probability < 0.01 end)
    |> Enum.map(fn {probability, word} ->
      %{step: "#{index} -> #{step.chosen}", word: word, probability: probability}
    end)
  end)

Vl.new(width: 640, height: 60 * length(Enum.uniq_by(steps, & &1.step)), title: "per-step distributions")
|> Vl.data_from_values(steps)
|> Vl.mark(:bar)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "probability", type: :quantitative)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "word", type: :nominal, sort: "-x")
|> Vl.encode_field(:row, "step", type: :nominal, sort: nil)
```

There is no state carried between those rows and no plan. Each one re-reads the
whole prefix from scratch, which is wasteful and is also why the model can never
contradict something it has already said without seeing it again.

## What is not here

No autodiff. No tensors. No tokenizer. No GPU. No hex package in any of the
model code, and `mix.exs` has an empty dependency list.

What is here is a decoder-only transformer with learned positional embeddings,
scaled dot-product attention, a causal mask, residual connections, RMSNorm, a
position-wise MLP, cross-entropy loss, backpropagation written out
explicitly and checked against finite differences, and temperature
sampling.

Every one of those is the same thing a frontier model does. The difference is
thirteen orders of magnitude and a tokenizer.
